- How would you optimize the inner max to O(n) using a deque when k is large?
- How would you reconstruct the actual partition that yields the maximum sum?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
